if $\Phi\subset\mathrm{Fl}(A)$, let
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Phi^*&=\{u\in\mathrm{Fl}(A)\mid u\text{ has the LLP with respect to }\Phi\}\\
\Phi_*&=\{u\in\mathrm{Fl}(A),\ \text{\rule{5em}{.3pt}}\ \mathrm{RLP}\ \text{\rule{8em}{.3pt}}\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
$\Phi$ is called left saturated if $\Phi=\Psi^*$ for some $\Psi$, i.e. $\Phi=(\Phi_*)^*$,
\inserted{$\Psi$} right saturated if \deleted{it is} $\Psi=\Phi_*$ for some $\Phi$ i.e. $\Psi=(\Psi^*)_*$.

Left saturation, right saturation,
\[
\Phi\subset\deleted{\Phi}(\Phi_*)^*,\quad \Psi\subset(\Psi^*)_*,
\]
Associations $\Phi\mapsto\unsure{\Phi|\Psi}_*^{\deleted{*}}$, $\Psi\mapsto\unsure{\Psi|\Phi}^{*}_{\deleted{*}}$ are \unsure{order} reversing bijections between left saturated and right saturated subsets.

\margin{Right saturation $\Psi$ $\Rightarrow$ stability by composition, base change, \unsure{retracts}. dually for left saturation.}

The \uline{factorization property} for a pair $(\Phi,\Psi)$ of mutually orthogonal subsets of $\mathrm{Fl}(A)$ $(\Phi\subset\Psi^*\text{ i.e. }\Psi\subset\Phi_*)$, is the property \deleted{that}
\[
\forall f\in\mathrm{Fl}(A)\quad\exists\text{ factorization }f=pi,\quad\text{with }i\in\Phi,p\in\Psi.
\]
\deleted{Let $\unsure{\Phi},\unsure{\Psi}$ be the left and right saturations respectively}

\deleted{\unsure{Prop.}} \deleted{Let}
\uline{Proposition} Assume factorization property holds \inserted{(for orthogonal pair $(\Phi,\Psi)$)}.
Then $\Phi=\Psi^*$ iff $\Phi$ stable by direct factors. Dually $\Psi=\Phi_*$ iff $\Psi$ stable by retractions. Hence $(\Phi,\Psi)$ \unsure{are} dual pair of subsets of $\mathrm{Fl}(A)$ (localizers) iff \deleted{and} \deleted{(they are orthogonal} both $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ are stable by direct factors.

\uline{Quillen pair} $(\Phi,\Psi)$:
\diagram{\begin{array}{l}
\text{dual pair}+\text{factorization}\\
\Updownarrow\\
\text{orthogonal pair}+\text{factorization}+\text{stability of }\Phi,\Psi\text{ by direct factors}.
\end{array}}

